{"name":"Finger Banana","hex":"#e1c12f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"finger","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A slender jointed extremity of the human hand, (often) exclusive of the thumb, and the corresponding part, irrespective of including a thumb, in many other animals.","Similar or similar-looking extremities in other animals, particularly:\n\nThe lower, smaller segment of an arthropod claw.\nOne of the supporting structures of wings in birds, bats, etc. evolved from earlier toes or fingers.\nOne of the slender bony structures before the pectoral fins of gurnards and sea robins (Triglidae)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To identify or point out; to blame for something.","To report to or identify for the authorities; to inform on."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/finger"},{"term":"banana","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The elongated curved tropical fruit of a banana plant, which grows in bunches and has a creamy flesh and a smooth skin. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","In particular, the sweet, yellow fruit of the Cavendish banana cultivar, which may be eaten raw, as distinct from e.g. a plantain for cooking."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Curved like a banana, especially of a ball in flight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/banana"}],"entries":[{"title":"Finger","blocks":[{"text":"A finger is a prominent, multi-jointed digit on the forelimbs of tetrapod vertebrate animals with prehensile extremities such as humans and other primates, and is typically covered with a keratinized nail to protect the end. Most tetrapods have five digits (pentadactyly), digits that are significantly shorter than the metacarpal/metatarsals, are typically referred to as toes; while digits that are notably elongated are called fingers. In humans, the fingers are opposable and have with two or three phalanges with fairly good flexional range of motion, serving as an important organ of tactile sensation and fine movements, which are crucial to the dexterity of the hands and the ability to grasp and manipulate objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Human_fingers_both_sides_2.jpg/330px-Human_fingers_both_sides_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finger"},{"title":"Banana","blocks":[{"text":"A banana is an elongated, edible fruit that is botanically a berry produced by several kinds of large treelike herbaceous flowering plants in the genus Musa. In some countries, cooking bananas are called plantains, distinguishing them from dessert bananas. The fruit is variable in size, color and firmness, but is usually elongated and curved, with soft flesh rich in starch covered with a peel, which may have a variety of colors when ripe. It grows upward in clusters near the top of the plant. Almost all modern edible seedless (parthenocarp) cultivated bananas come from two wild species – Musa acuminata and Musa balbisiana, or their hybrids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Bananavarieties.jpg/330px-Bananavarieties.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banana"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg/500px-Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg/1280px-Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg/500px-Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Radiograph of Type 1 Syndactyly","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fil